in our last episode we covered best  practices for deploying and using cloud  monitoring today we'll start looking at  best practices that you can use to get  the most value out of cloud logging  we'll cover how to ingest and store logs  and we'll cover the best ways to query  logs and manage logging costs in our  next episode welcome to engineering for  reliability with google cloud  before we can do anything with logs at  scale our services need to create them  and they need to be ingested in cloud  logging  we covered the sources of logs in google  cloud in the previous episode but let's  quickly review  there's nothing you need to do to  control how logs are emitted from the  platform and network  but when writing your own logs there are  some best practices you should follow to  get the most value out of that data  this is where structured logging comes  in  consider a log entry that looks like  this  where the entire payload message is in a  single text payload field  if you want to get any data out of that  field you have to use regular  expressions or other string processing  methods  using a structured logging approach like  this to create log entries with the json  payload field that then has nested  fields under it allows you to query and  analyze log data much easier  every field within json payload can be  used to search in query logs just like  other fields  you can even add it to the logs field  explorer to enable easily grouping and  searching by that field later  but how do you get your application  locks to look like that  if your logs are written by the cloud  logging or the cloud apps agents use one  of the supported parsers to emit  structured logs or write your own parser  if needed  if you're using the cloud logging api  send the full log entry structure with  the json payload  finally if you're using either the cloud  logging library or a third-party login  library in your code  make sure you're using them in the way  that emits structured logs  we'll link to the documentation for each  of these in the episode notes  writing logs in a way that allows you to  make the best use of the data in them is  great but what's the best way to store  them to balance security retention and  access  let's have a look at log centralization  configuring retention managing access  and using exports  first keeping your logs in a central  place helps you manage all your logs  consistently and efficiently  we talked about using log buckets in the  previous episode but remember that for  each cloud project billing account  folder and organization  cloud logging automatically creates two  log buckets required and default  you can create additional buckets and  configure log syncs that send logs from  multiple projects to those buckets  it's often advisable to do this for  audit logs as an example  your business needs and requirements  will likely govern how long you need to  retain logs for  you can configure the retention period  for any user defined log bucket and also  for the default lock bucket  simply edit the bucket under log storage  and set your preferred retention period  up to 10 years  you can also control where the logs in  the bucket are stored you also have the  option to set the location to global if  you don't need to specify where your  logs are physically stored  there are two mechanisms you can use to  control access to logs and buckets the  first one is via iam permissions we'll  link to the detailed guide in the  episode nodes but a user will at least  need the logging viewer role to be able  to read logs in the project  the second mechanism to control log  access is log views which provides you  with an advanced and granular way to  control access to the logs in your log  buckets  for example consider a scenario in which  you store all of your organization's  logs in the central project  in this case you might want to control  which projects different users can view  logs from  using custom log views you can give one  user access to logs only from a single  project  while you give another user access to  logs from all the projects  finally you may need to export logs to a  third-party system such as splunk  you can do that using a push-based  method through the splunk http event  connector or pull-based method using the  splunk add-on for google cloud platform  we covered best practices for ingesting  and storing logs in cloud logging and  controlling log access retention and  exports  i hope these recommendations help you  keep your services reliable and your  users happy in our next episode we'll  tackle best practices for analyzing logs  and managing logging costs thanks so  much for watching today don't forget to  like and subscribe to never miss out on  more engineering for reliability with  google cloud see you soon
 

